<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="sec_5_5">
    <title>The Natural Base e</title>

    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                After completing this section, you should be able to do the following.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Recognize the natural base <m>e</m> and the natural exponential function <m>y = e^x</m>.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Convert an exponential function between the standard form <m>A \cdot b^t</m> and the base-<m>e</m> form <m>A \cdot e^{kt}</m>.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Distinguish between the continuous growth rate and the percent growth rate of an exponential function.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <p>
        A commonly used base in the context of exponential functions is the <em>Euler constant</em>. The constant is denoted by the letter <m>e</m> and its approximate value is
        <md>
            e \approx 2.718281827 \dots
        </md>
        The constant <m>e</m> is an irrational number which, much like <m>\pi</m>, appears in various areas of mathematics. It is sufficient for you to remember that <m>e \approx 2.718</m> or even
        <md>
            e \approx 2.71
        </md>
        Your calculator will give you a better approximation of <m>e</m>. A precise definition of <m>e</m> is complicated, and will not be covered in this text.
    </p>

    <p>
        We call <m>e</m> the <alert>natural base</alert> and, when used as the base of an exponential function, we get the so-called <alert>natural exponential</alert> function <m>y = e^x</m>.
        Since <m>e\gt 1</m>, the graph of a natural exponential function is increasing, just as any other exponential function with growth factor larger than <m>1</m>:
    </p>

    <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_5_5_1">
        <latex-image label="5_5_1_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
xmin=-3,xmax=3,
ymin=0,ymax=9,
xtick={-3,...,3},
ytick={0,...,9},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
x post scale = 1.2
]
\addplot[domain=-3:3] {e^x};% node[pos=0.4,above left] {$y=e^x$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
    </image>

    <p>
        In fact, <m>2 \lt e \lt 3</m> so the graph of <m>y = e^x</m> is between the graphs of <m>y = 2^x</m> and <m>y = 3^x</m>:
    </p>

    <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_5_5_2">
        <latex-image label="5_5_2_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
xmin=-1,xmax=2,
ymin=0,ymax=9,
xtick={-3,...,3},
ytick={0,...,9},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
x post scale = 1.2,
legend pos=north west,
]
\addplot[domain=-3:3] {e^x}; %node[pos=0.3,below right] {$y=e^x$};
\addlegendentry{$e^x$};
\addplot[domain=-3:3,red] {2^x};% node[pos=0.4,below right] {$y=2^x$};
\addlegendentry{$2^x$};
\addplot[domain=-3:3,orange] {3^x};% node[pos=0.3,above left] {$y=3^x$};
\addlegendentry{$3^x$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
    </image>

    <p>
        In most applications, especially in life sciences, people tend to rewrite <alert>all</alert> exponential functions in terms of the natural base <m>e</m>. How can this be done? Suppose we have an exponential function <m>f(x) = A \cdot b^x</m> where <m>b</m> is any positive number. By the graph above, we can see that there exists a constant <m>k</m> such that
        <md>
            b = e^k.
        </md>
        This means that one can rewrite any exponential function as
        <md>
            f(x) = A \cdot b^x = A \cdot (e^k)^x = A \cdot e^{k x}.
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        The graph indicates that when <m>b \gt 1</m>, the constant <m>k</m> is positive; likewise when <m>b \lt 1</m>, the constant <m>k</m> is negative.
    </p>

    <theorem>
        <title>Exponential Function in Terms of Base <m>e</m></title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                Any exponential function <m>Q(t) = A \cdot b^t</m> can be written in the form
                <md>
                    Q(t) = A \cdot e^{k t}
                </md>
                where <m>k</m> is the constant satisfying that <m>e^k = b</m>. We have:
            </p>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>b \gt 1</m>, then <m>k \gt 0</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>b \lt 1</m>, then <m>k \lt 0</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The constant <m>k</m> is called the <term>continuous growth rate</term>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            We can convert from <m>Q(t) = A \cdot e^{k t}</m> form to the form <m>Q(t) = A \cdot b^t</m> by setting the growth factor <m>b = e^k</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
        <alert>Remark:</alert>
        The constant <m>k</m> in the formula <m>Q(t) = A \cdot e^{k t}</m> is called the the <alert>continuous growth rate</alert> or, when expressed as a percent, the <alert>continuous percent growth rate</alert>.
        The relevance of this terminology will become more clear if you go on to study calculus.
    </p>

    <p>
        It is important to keep in mind the following facts.
    </p>

    <p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The value of the constant <m>k</m> depends on the units of <m>t</m><mdash/>years, days, hours etc. Hence, we will sometimes say the <q>continuous annual growth rate</q> <m>k</m> or the <q>continuous daily growth rate</q> <m>k</m> etc., to emphasize the units of <m>t</m> unless the units of <m>t</m> are clear from the context.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    The values of the continuous percent growth rate <m>k</m> and the percent growth rate <m>r</m> that appear in the formula
                    <md>
                        Q(t) = A \cdot b^t = A (1 + r)^t
                    </md>
                    are <alert>different</alert>, although their values are <em>often close</em>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
        <alert>Note:</alert>
        Any exponential in base-<m>e</m> form, say <m>Q(t) = A \cdot e^{k t}</m>, can easily be converted to standard form <m>Q(t) = A \cdot b^t</m> by taking <m>b = e^k</m>. Going the other way around, that is, finding the exact value of <m>k</m> for a given <m>b</m> requires solving the following equation for <m>k</m>:
        <md>
            e^k = b.
        </md>
        Since the unknown <m>k</m> is in the exponent, the equation cannot be solved algebraically until Chapter 6 since it requires logarithms.
    </p>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The value of investment, <m>V(t)</m>, is given by
                <md>
                    V(t) = 2500 e^{0.035 t}
                </md>
                where <m>t</m> is in years. Give the initial value and the continuous growth rate. Then, rewrite the function in the form
                <md>
                    V(t) = A \cdot b^t
                </md>
                and compare the percent growth rate and the continuous percent growth rate.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                From the base-<m>e</m> form, the <alert>continuous</alert> growth rate is <m>k = 0.035 = 3.5\%</m> and the initial value of the investment is <m>A=2500</m>.
                Notice that
                <md>
                    V(t) = 2500 e^{0.035 t} = 2500 \left( e^{0.035} \right)^t = 2500 \cdot 1.0356^t
                </md>
                as <m>e^{0.035} \approx 1.0356</m>. Hence, the growth factor is <m>b=1.0356</m> and the annual percent growth rate is <m>r=0.0356 = 3.56\%</m>.
                This shows that the continuous growth rate and the annual percent growth rate are <em>close</em> but not <em>equal</em>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A patient treated for thyroid cancer is given an injection of 10 <m>\mu</m>g of Iodine-131. Let <m>I(t)</m> be the amount of Iodine-131 left in the patient's body <m>t</m> days after the injection.
                Assume that the <alert>continuous</alert> daily decay rate of Iodine-131 is <m>8.66\%</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find a formula for <m>I(t)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Convert the formula to the form <m>I(t) = 10 \cdot b^t</m>. Compare the daily percent growth rate and the continuous growth rate.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The <alert>continuous</alert> growth rate is given as <m>k = -8.66\% = -0.0866</m>. Hence, it will be easier to find a formula for <m>I(t)</m> in the form <m>I(t) = A e^{k t}</m>.
                            The initial amount is <m>A = 10</m>, so
                            <md>
                                I(t) = 10 e^{-0.0866 t}.
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            To convert to the form <m>I(t) = 10 \cdot b^t</m>, take <m>b = e^k = e^{-0.0866} = 0.917</m>.
                            Therefore,
                            <md>
                                I(t) = 10 (0.917)^t.
                            </md>
                            The daily percent growth rate is <m>r = 0.917 - 1 = -0.083 = -8.3\%</m>.
                            Observe that the two rates, <m>k=-8.66\%</m> and <m>r=-8.3\%</m>, differ significantly.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="exercises/ex_5_5.ptx"/>

</section>
